FIVE_MINUTES = '''“The purpose of this application is to make portfolio decisions traceable to delivery facts. Through Sprint 07 it has working identity, project delivery records, resource capacity, versioned maturity assessments and four command-center views. The wider roadmap includes AI, but I would not describe the current scoring as AI. Its calculations are ordinary, inspectable Python and Decimal arithmetic.

The runtime has a Next.js web process, FastAPI API, PostgreSQL database, Redis and a Linux Celery worker. PostgreSQL is the source of truth. Redis serves shared identity rate limits and the worker broker/result path. The worker currently runs a checkpoint diagnostic, not durable business workflows. Private evidence files live outside PostgreSQL, with metadata and permissions in the database.

Let me trace project creation. The React RecordEditor gathers local form values and builds a discriminated ProjectInput. It sends a create UUID as an idempotency key. Shared transport obtains a CSRF token, sends it as a header and supplies the organization whose data the user has loaded. The request goes to a fixed same-origin Next proxy and then to FastAPI.

Middleware checks unsafe-request Origin and CSRF and establishes a request ID. FastAPI validates the body and resolves Auth. That dependency verifies the JWT, looks up a live database session, checks expiry and revocation, resolves the active membership and sets transaction-local user, organization and role values. Those values drive PostgreSQL row policies. The browser's organization header is just a stale-tab check; it does not decide authority.

The delivery command takes an organization advisory lock and checks live authority again after waiting. Admin can create a project; a Project Manager needs manage access to the portfolio. It checks organization setup, active references and program/portfolio consistency. SQLAlchemy Core executes an INSERT RETURNING on the same AsyncConnection. It then writes before/after audit and the retry receipt. A PM creator also receives project manage access in that transaction. Successful completion commits; failure rolls back the database effects. The response is shaped through RecordView and the frontend reloads the authorized list.

For an update, the UI sends the revision it loaded in If-Match. If two users load revision four, the first accepted edit creates revision five. The second cannot silently overwrite it: it fails with 412 after the lock and current revision check. The SQL update also includes the expected revision. The form preserves unsaved values so the user can reload and reconcile. A missing revision is 428; a conflicting retry key is 409. Those statuses represent different failure causes.

History matters because this is more than CRUD. Baselines freeze earlier plans. Spending corrections append an exact reversal rather than rewriting the original amount. Sprint closure freezes completed points, so later work-item edits do not rewrite past velocity. These protections exist in application rules and restricted database privileges.

Two calculations show how the same design handles incomplete evidence. A four-hour calendar over five weekdays gives twenty available hours. Two three-hour allocations each day give thirty planned hours, or 150 percent. Leave reduces availability while planned work stays visible. However, a PM who sees only some projects cannot infer whole-resource utilization. For assessments, frozen numeric rubrics produce weighted scores; required text affects completion but is not semantically scored by a model.

The command center uses one authorized snapshot for four views and JSON download. Its risk combines schedule, financial and staffing components with fixed weights. If one is missing, the result is unknown. Human-reported Green remains separate from evidence-derived health, and an overdue critical milestone can expose a discrepancy. Source timestamps, coverage and facts accompany the number.

Finally, the evidence is local: source, migration history, real database/security tests and browser acceptance records. I would discuss the coarse organization lock, repeated queries and local object storage as tradeoffs to measure and evolve, rather than claim the system is already production-scale or AI-complete.”'''

FIFTEEN_MINUTES = '''Use this spoken script with the indicated code pauses; the pauses are for walking through real functions and questions, not padding the answer.

“I will start with what has actually been implemented. This checkpoint is Sprint 07, supported by the latest handoff, Git savepoint and migration head 0006_command_center. The application solves an operational PPM problem: project status, financials, workload and assessment results need an authoritative source and an explanation. A Green status is a human report. It should not hide an overdue critical gate or unsupported capacity claim. The current product therefore connects editable facts to scoped, explainable metrics.

Architecturally it is a modular monolith with separate web, API and worker processes. Next.js supplies route wrappers, React interaction and a fixed server proxy. FastAPI supplies request processing and domain routers. PostgreSQL owns canonical identity and business records. Redis provides a shared identity limiter and the Celery broker/result backend. The local object adapter holds private evidence bytes. There is no implemented vector store, provider gateway or agent layer in this checkpoint. That distinction is essential because the project name and original specification have a broader scope than current source.

Let us look at a concrete create-project command. In RecordEditor.save, values from the form become a complete typed payload with kind=projects. One UUID per create editor is the logical command key. The shared transport obtains the readable CSRF token if necessary and sets the CSRF and active-organization headers. Cookies are browser-managed. The Next proxy forwards only named headers and a bounded request body to API_ORIGIN. The browser cannot supply its own upstream URL. It also forwards the upstream Set-Cookie headers separately, which matters for login and refresh.”

**Code pause:** open `RecordEditor.save`, `identity-client.tsx:transport` and the Next proxy. Identify the body, idempotency header, organization precondition and response reload.

“FastAPI middleware gives the request a UUID, rejects unsafe methods without the expected Origin and CSRF match, and later records sanitized status/timing. The framework parses body and parameters and resolves Auth. Auth is a reusable dependency, implemented as an async generator called principal. It checks the access token's signature, fixed algorithm, issuer, audience, expiry and required claims, then queries the database session. A correctly signed token is still rejected if that session is expired, revoked or has switched organization. Active membership and verified user status are also required.

The dependency opens an engine.begin transaction and sets user, organization and role using transaction-local PostgreSQL settings. It yields a Principal containing that same connection to the endpoint. This is the important correction from a Spring/JPA mental model: our domain code uses SQLAlchemy Core expressions and AsyncConnection, not a Session with managed entities and automatic dirty checking. A changed Python dictionary does not flush itself into the database.

Next, delivery.create takes the organization lock. It checks the organization header only as a precondition against the server-resolved tenant. After waiting for the advisory lock, it checks membership and session again. Otherwise an actor could be authorized before waiting and continue after their access was revoked. Under the lock it looks for the scoped idempotency receipt. Reuse with a different payload or resource is rejected. An identical replay rechecks current authority and reads the target again.

For a new create, write checks the action, role and grant. A Project Manager needs manage access to the portfolio. The server verifies active references, the program belongs to the selected portfolio, and required organization settings exist. Pydantic has already checked the public shape and date constraints, but these relationship checks require database state. PostgreSQL still applies constraints and policies during execution. These layers solve different problems.

The Core insert returns the new row. The code supplies tenant, ID, creator, timestamps and revision itself; it never lets the body become tenant authority. It appends a before/after audit and create receipt on the same connection, and adds a project manage grant for a PM creator. Transaction completion makes these effects durable together. The result is shaped into a safe RecordView, serialized to JSON and causes the frontend to reload scoped data.”

**Code pause:** walk `principal`, `delivery.lock`, `create` and the INSERT/UPDATE section of `write`. Predict the outcome if an exception occurs after the project insert and before receipt insertion.

“For security, authentication, authorization and tenant isolation must be separated. Authentication identifies a live user. Application authorization decides whether this action is allowed. Tenant isolation and project visibility also apply in SQL. Policies use current_setting for the transaction's user, organization and role. The runtime role is a non-owner without BYPASSRLS. Missing tenant context cannot simply read all tenant rows. Composite foreign keys include organization and sometimes project IDs so a valid UUID from another scope cannot become a valid relationship.

The global identity control plane is a deliberate exception to tenant RLS. Login and challenge completion need narrow pre-authentication lookups of users, sessions and hashed credentials. Organizations, memberships, audits and domain facts have their applicable policies. I would not say every table has identical RLS, or that the migration owner is unable to bypass it. The application role and server context-setting code are trust boundaries.

Passwords use bcrypt cost twelve with explicit UTF-8 byte limits. Access tokens last ten minutes; live sessions have a fixed seven-day limit. Refresh credentials are random and stored as hashes. Refresh locks the session and rotates once. Reusing a consumed token revokes the session. The implementation commits that revocation before returning an error; otherwise rollback would erase the protection. The browser coalesces refresh within a page, but independent tabs can still race and require login again. This is a concrete security-versus-convenience tradeoff.

Concurrency has a similarly explicit contract. Two project editors read revision seven. The first edit takes the organization lock, validates seven and writes eight. The second sees eight and receives 412. A missing precondition is 428. The final UPDATE also predicates on revision. This prevents lost edits without claiming an automatic merge. The organization lock additionally coordinates final-Admin changes and cross-record domain checks, but it is coarse and limits concurrent work within one tenant.”

**Code pause:** inspect the project read policy, `context(...true)` and the two-editor test. Explain why an owner-only SQL test would be inadequate RLS evidence.

“The domain is not only current mutable records. Financial corrections are append-only exact negative reversals. Baselines freeze a project and selected children. Closed sprint points are frozen at closure rather than recomputed later. Audit has actor, timestamp, request ID and before/after data, while operational logs have route, status and duration. This is application-role immutable history, not a cryptographically tamper-proof or fully event-sourced system.

Capacity illustrates how data visibility affects a calculation. For each local date, calculate chooses effective availability and an immutable calendar. Holiday hours are zero and leave is subtracted with a lower bound of zero. Allocations count on scheduled weekdays even if leave makes the person unavailable, so conflict stays visible. A five-day four-hour schedule has twenty available hours. Two three-hour daily allocations have thirty planned, giving 150 percent. Two days of full leave leave twelve available hours, 250 percent and eighteen hours of daily excess.

Excess is summed per day because spare capacity on another day does not cancel an overload. A missing calendar means unknown capacity. A scoped Project Manager cannot turn visible allocations into a claim about the whole resource. Actual effort is additionally sensitive: even Admin requires an explicit effort capability, and utilization needs complete daily attestations. Amending effort invalidates completeness. Team signals suppress cohorts below five on any selected day and are not medical diagnoses.

Assessments use a different form of reproducibility. The framework is editable JSON, but publication freezes a version. Assignments bind to that version and to different respondent and reviewer accounts. A boolean answer becomes zero or one; a rubric level becomes its normalized distance between published minimum and maximum. Dimension scores divide weighted numeric values by answered numeric weight, and dimension weights form overall score. Text answers affect completeness but not numeric scoring. A missing required answer can coexist with a provisional draft score, but blocks submit or complete. Reviewer overrides retain reasons and raw answers. None of this is semantic AI scoring.”

**Calculation pause:** derive 87.5 from rubric weight one at 0.5 plus boolean weight three at 1. Then set the rubric override to zero to get 75. Explain why weighted answer coverage and per-question evidence coverage have different denominators.

“The command center consumes a shared scoped snapshot. Schedule risk comes from working-day delay versus a frozen baseline duration. Financial risk comes from forecast overrun versus approved budget. Capacity risk comes from next-28-day excess versus available capacity with full scope. They are clamped and combined with weights forty, thirty-five and twenty-five percent. If one required component is absent the combined value is unknown. Health points are one hundred minus risk, and organization thresholds define the category. Those points are not a learned failure probability.

Reported health stays separate. A Green report with an overdue critical milestone shows discrepancy even when the composite cannot be computed. The DTO carries facts, unit, period, source age and coverage. Freshness uses the oldest contributor rather than the moment the snapshot was calculated. The date selector filters period activity while current health remains current state. It does not reconstruct arbitrary historical dashboards. Export re-fetches with current authority.

Operationally, Compose starts database and Redis, then owner migration, then healthy API, then web and worker. Named volumes preserve local data; they do not prove disaster recovery. The only current worker task is a database checkpoint. Redis AOF and a running Celery process do not establish outbox, durable job leases, retries or recovery of external effects. Those are planned Sprint 08 work. Evidence upload also has a boundary worth acknowledging: atomic file replacement is not atomic with the database, so rollback may leave orphan bytes.

Testing covers meaningful negative cases as well as happy paths: stale revisions, cross-tenant references, pool-context reuse, refresh replay, independent review, privacy suppression and metric reconciliation. The repository defines a CI pipeline with static, contract, real-stack, browser and persistence checks. The acceptance evidence here is local; I would not claim a hosted green run, deployment or complete accessibility certification without that evidence.

For scaling I would start with measurements. The recorded tiny snapshot already uses forty-eight SQL statements. Adding replicas increases connection pools and does not remove the organization lock. At ten times scale I would batch repeated queries, measure plans and pool budgets, implement durable jobs and establish object retention/backup operations. At larger scale I would evaluate scoped projections and finer-grained coordination while preserving immediate revocation, composite invariants and explicit missing data. Future RAG would need versioned authorized ingestion, provider evaluation and typed tools; a model could propose a change but should still use our existing authorized revision-safe command boundary.”

**Closing discussion:** identify which statements describe current source and which describe proposed improvements. If asked about personal ownership, state Backend Engineering and substantiate the contribution rather than claiming every feature in this architecture. '''
